{:eval/lib :kotoba-ui, :eval/axis :axis/deference, :eval/layer :llm-judge, :eval/score 4.30, :eval/judge "llm-judge-1", :eval/run-id "design-quality-20260713-0544", :eval/at "2026-07-13T05:44:03Z", :eval/seq 53, :eval/note "The API design actively pushes toward minimal chrome: unlayered app CSS always wins over library CSS by cascade-layer construction (not just convention), :wide opts a section out of the readable-column constraint, and scaffolds are structural-only (flex/grid primitives) rather than opinionated visual components. The one default that leans decorative is hero's radial accent wash, but it's documented as 'subtle' (12% color-mix), token-driven (follows theme accent, not a hardcoded gradient), and confined to one scaffold rather than pervasive. Nothing forces heavy chrome; a caller can render bare panels/stacks with zero decoration."}
{:eval/lib :kotoba-ui, :eval/axis :axis/deference, :eval/layer :llm-judge, :eval/score 4.00, :eval/judge "llm-judge-2", :eval/run-id "design-quality-20260713-0544", :eval/at "2026-07-13T05:44:03Z", :eval/seq 54, :eval/note "The library structurally privileges content over chrome: agent-guide.md rule 3 states unlayered app CSS always wins regardless of source order/specificity (verified in theme.cljc/style.cljc — all library CSS is wrapped in @layer kotoba.hig / kotoba.glass), section() defaults to a readable 680px column, hero's accent wash is a subtle 12% color-mix rather than an opaque decorative background, and nothing forces motion or heavy chrome into the required path (->page emits only meta/title/theme-css/data-appearance). Docked slightly because a few shell defaults are still fixed opinions baked into CSS rather than opt-in (hero's radial wash and section's hairline top-border render unconditionally unless you avoid those components), so \"minimal chrome\" requires choosing not to use hero/section rather than the defaults themselves being minimal."}
