{:eval/lib :kotoba-ui, :eval/axis :axis/depth, :eval/layer :llm-judge, :eval/score 4.00, :eval/judge "llm-judge-1", :eval/run-id "design-quality-20260713-0544", :eval/at "2026-07-13T05:44:03Z", :eval/seq 56, :eval/note "kotoba-ui itself doesn't mint new elevation/motion vocabulary, but it deliberately re-exports the layered material's depth primitives wholesale: resolve-tokens/resolve-dark-tokens/spring-linear-easing, component-css (which the design.md explicly maps to 'Motion' — overlay enter/exit, spring settle, press morph), and glass tokens keyed by :surface/:elevation/:specular/:accent per docs/design.md's taxonomy table. The shell layer adds a concrete z-order signal (sticky app-nav at z-index:10 over app-body) rather than a flat stacking model. This is coherent layering communicated through composition/re-export rather than duplicated locally, which is appropriate for its role as the wiring layer, so it doesn't reach a 5."}
{:eval/lib :kotoba-ui, :eval/axis :axis/depth, :eval/layer :llm-judge, :eval/score 3.50, :eval/judge "llm-judge-2", :eval/run-id "design-quality-20260713-0544", :eval/at "2026-07-13T05:44:03Z", :eval/seq 57, :eval/note "There is real layering signal — an explicit, canonically-ordered cascade-layer system (kotoba.hig, kotoba.glass declared once in theme-css), a documented sticky app-nav with z-index, and design.md names elevation/surface/specular as a category mapped to liquid-glass tokens (:surface/:elevation referenced via appkit/uikit example, e.g. :elevation :floating) plus a Motion row (spring settle, press morph). However, within kotoba-ui's own source the elevation/motion token vocabulary is not defined here — it's inherited/re-exported from liquid-glass-ui (component-css is opaque re-export, \"no separate motion API\"), and the one internal z-order value (:z-index \"10\" for app-nav) is a bare literal, not a named depth token. So kotoba-ui documents depth clearly at the taxonomy level but doesn't itself own a graduated depth/elevation token model."}
