{:eval/lib :liquid-glass-ui, :eval/axis :axis/clarity, :eval/layer :llm-judge, :eval/score 4.567, :eval/stdev 0.094, :eval/judge "llm-judge-mean", :eval/run-id "design-quality-20260713-0544", :eval/at "2026-07-13T05:44:03Z", :eval/seq 71, :eval/note "mean of 3 independent judges"}
{:eval/lib :liquid-glass-ui, :eval/axis :axis/deference, :eval/layer :llm-judge, :eval/score 4.00, :eval/judge "llm-judge-1", :eval/run-id "design-quality-20260713-0544", :eval/at "2026-07-13T05:44:03Z", :eval/seq 72, :eval/note "The library architecturally defers to consumer content by design: it ships CSS inside a `kotoba.glass` cascade layer so any unlayered app CSS automatically outranks it regardless of specificity/order, explicitly killing the \"compound-selector override war\" pattern documented as a real prior problem (net-babiniku's ~100 lines of `.liquid-glass__toolbar.app-toolbar`). Components carry no inline visual style, only class + var() references, so overriding is a token problem not a component-code problem. It does default panel/toolbar/etc. to non-flat surfaces (:regular/:raised, toolbar defaults to :thick), which is somewhat chrome-forward, but :clear/:flat opt-outs exist on the same API for minimal-chrome cases, and reduced-motion is respected everywhere by default."}
{:eval/lib :liquid-glass-ui, :eval/axis :axis/deference, :eval/layer :llm-judge, :eval/score 3.20, :eval/judge "llm-judge-2", :eval/run-id "design-quality-20260713-0544", :eval/at "2026-07-13T05:44:03Z", :eval/seq 73, :eval/note "The library does offer a real \"minimal chrome\" path — :clear surface, :flat elevation, and unlayered app CSS always outranks the library's @layer kotoba.glass rules by design (documented cascade contract) — so a consumer CAN keep chrome minimal without specificity fights. But defaults lean decorative: panel/button/etc. default to :regular surface + :raised elevation (not :flat/:clear), every glass-surface component gets a specular ::before overlay, rim-light insets, backdrop blur+saturate+brightness, and press/settle motion baked in unless the caller actively opts down. As a dedicated \"glass skin\" library this is somewhat inherent to its purpose, but the opinionated non-flat defaults do push toward chrome-forward output rather than deferring to content by default."}
{:eval/lib :liquid-glass-ui, :eval/axis :axis/deference, :eval/layer :llm-judge, :eval/score 2.80, :eval/judge "llm-judge-3", :eval/run-id "design-quality-20260713-0544", :eval/at "2026-07-13T05:44:03Z", :eval/seq 74, :eval/note "The library's entire purpose is a decorative glass material — nearly every surface gets backdrop-filter blur+saturate+brightness, a specular ::before highlight, a two-tone rim-light inset shadow, and an elevation drop-shadow by default (style.cljc glass-surface-components list, base-rules); defaults (:regular surface, :raised elevation) are moderate-to-heavy chrome, not minimal, and there's no zero-chrome default variant beyond :clear/:flat which callers must opt into explicitly. Architecturally it does defer well to app content: the `@layer kotoba.hig, kotoba.glass` contract is explicitly designed so unlayered app CSS always outranks the library's rules regardless of source order or specificity (style.cljc layer-order/layered-css, README 'Cascade contract'), retiring ~100 lines of specificity-war overrides a consumer previously needed. That's a genuine deference-to-content mechanism at the architecture level even though the material itself pushes decoration by design."}
