{:eval/lib :liquid-glass-ui, :eval/axis :axis/depth, :eval/layer :llm-judge, :eval/score 5.00, :eval/judge "llm-judge-1", :eval/run-id "design-quality-20260713-0544", :eval/at "2026-07-13T05:44:03Z", :eval/seq 76, :eval/note "This is the library's core competency: four named elevation levels (:flat/:raised/:overlay/:floating) each with distinct multi-layer box-shadow, three surface tiers (:clear/:regular/:thick) with distinct blur/saturate/tint, explicit z-order via isolation/position, an asymmetric top/bottom rim-light token pair documented as deliberately modeling a lit edge (not a flat panel), and a whole named motion vocabulary (:press/:settle/:overlay-enter/:overlay-exit/:spring) including a generated damped-spring linear() easing derived from an actual physics step-response formula rather than a magic curve. Layering and motion are treated as first-class, well-reasoned token groups, not afterthoughts."}
{:eval/lib :liquid-glass-ui, :eval/axis :axis/depth, :eval/layer :llm-judge, :eval/score 5.00, :eval/judge "llm-judge-2", :eval/run-id "design-quality-20260713-0544", :eval/at "2026-07-13T05:44:03Z", :eval/seq 77, :eval/note "Strong, coherent layering model: four named elevation levels (flat/raised/overlay/floating) each with distinct shadow tokens, a separate specular/rim token group (asymmetric top/bottom opacity to simulate a lit edge), isolation/z-index used deliberately (isolate on glass roots, z-index on gauge-label/pointer overlay), and a full motion vocabulary (press, settle, overlay-enter/exit, spring with a genuinely generated damped-spring linear() easing) — motion and depth are explicitly tied together (e.g. press squash + spring overshoot reads as physical glass). This is the most differentiated axis of the library."}
{:eval/lib :liquid-glass-ui, :eval/axis :axis/depth, :eval/layer :llm-judge, :eval/score 4.90, :eval/judge "llm-judge-3", :eval/run-id "design-quality-20260713-0544", :eval/at "2026-07-13T05:44:03Z", :eval/seq 78, :eval/note "Strong, coherent layering model: four named elevation levels (:flat/:raised/:overlay/:floating) each with a distinct shadow token, isolation:isolate + position:relative establishing stacking contexts, z-index used deliberately (gauge-label, pointer specular), and a full motion vocabulary (:press, :settle, :overlay-enter, :overlay-exit, :spring) with duration+easing pairs, including a generated damped-spring linear() easing (tokens.cljc spring-linear-easing, a pure fn sampling an analytic spring step-response rather than a magic curve). The rim-light token (asymmetric top/bottom opacity, documented as \"light source from above\") and the ink token's light/dark redeclaration are additional depth cues beyond a flat surface model. This is a well-differentiated elevation+motion system, not token soup."}
{:eval/lib :liquid-glass-ui, :eval/axis :axis/depth, :eval/layer :llm-judge, :eval/score 4.967, :eval/stdev 0.047, :eval/judge "llm-judge-mean", :eval/run-id "design-quality-20260713-0544", :eval/at "2026-07-13T05:44:03Z", :eval/seq 79, :eval/note "mean of 3 independent judges"}
{:eval/lib :liquid-glass-ui, :eval/axis :axis/consistency, :eval/layer :llm-judge, :eval/score 4.00, :eval/judge "llm-judge-1", :eval/run-id "design-quality-20260713-0544", :eval/at "2026-07-13T05:44:03Z", :eval/seq 80, :eval/note "Strong match on the parts of the baseline that apply to a material layer: CSS custom properties emitted under `:root`/`@layer`, and dark-mode overrides re-declare the SAME `--liquid-glass-*` var names inside a `prefers-color-scheme` media query rather than minting `-dark` variants — exactly the documented convention. The `@layer kotoba.hig, kotoba.glass` ordering also matches the stack's layering contract precisely, and the docs are explicit that liquid-glass.* is a layer apps should not require directly (consistent with kotoba-ui.core being the single app-facing entry point per the shared convention). Where it diverges: it does not expose the appkit/uikit-shaped `default-panel-opts` map + `(merge default-opts caller-opts)` pattern — `panel`/`list-view` instead take opts directly and build variant classes via `cond->`, functionally similar (in-function defaults, caller always wins) but a different literal shape a sibling-trained developer wouldn't immediately recognize."}
{:eval/lib :liquid-glass-ui, :eval/axis :axis/consistency, :eval/layer :llm-judge, :eval/score 3.60, :eval/judge "llm-judge-2", :eval/run-id "design-quality-20260713-0544", :eval/at "2026-07-13T05:44:03Z", :eval/seq 81, :eval/note "Matches the baseline exactly on the CSS-var mechanism (light tokens under :root, dark overrides redeclare the SAME custom-property name inside prefers-color-scheme rather than minting -dark variants) and on the cascade-layer discipline (@layer kotoba.hig, kotoba.glass, unlayered app CSS wins). It deliberately does NOT expose a single uikit.core/appkit.core-shaped entry namespace — but per its documented role as the bottom material layer (consumed by kotoba-ui.core, not required directly by apps), that's consistent with the stack's architecture rather than a violation of it. Where it diverges from the sibling shape is the opts pattern: appkit/uikit's default-panel-opts + (merge default-opts caller-opts) convention is not present here — panel/button/etc. just destructure opts inline with ad hoc cond/or defaults rather than an explicit default-opts map merged with caller opts, so a developer who learned that specific merge idiom would not immediately recognize this library's opts handling."}
{:eval/lib :liquid-glass-ui, :eval/axis :axis/consistency, :eval/layer :llm-judge, :eval/score 4.50, :eval/judge "llm-judge-3", :eval/run-id "design-quality-20260713-0544", :eval/at "2026-07-13T05:44:03Z", :eval/seq 82, :eval/note "Strong match to the stated baseline: token vars follow exactly the described dark-mode technique (dark-tokens is a partial override map applied under prefers-color-scheme, redeclaring the SAME --liquid-glass-* custom-property names rather than minting -dark variants — tokens.cljc lines 121-136, dark-css-variables). CSS ships inside '@layer kotoba.hig, kotoba.glass' exactly as the baseline describes. The panel component's opt vocabulary ({:surface :clear|:regular|:thick, :elevation ...}) is the literal vocabulary the baseline's appkit/uikit example opts map references, confirming this is the layer those sibling libraries build defaults on top of. It does not itself expose a single 'liquid_glass.core' entry namespace merging default-opts with caller-opts the way appkit/uikit do — but per its documented role as the bottom material layer (consumed by kotoba-ui.core, not required directly by apps), that is the expected shape rather than a deviation."}
