{:eval/lib :liquid-glass-ui, :eval/axis :axis/token-discipline-judged, :eval/layer :llm-judge, :eval/score 5.00, :eval/judge "llm-judge-1", :eval/run-id "design-quality-20260713-0544", :eval/at "2026-07-13T05:44:03Z", :eval/seq 84, :eval/note "Tokens are organized into clearly bounded, purpose-named groups (surface/elevation/specular/radius/motion/accent/lens/ink) with per-value rationale documented inline (e.g. why rim opacity is asymmetric top/bottom, why :thick exists for busy-content separation, why :ink exists and how it avoids illegible inherited text). dark-tokens is explicitly scoped to only the scheme-sensitive subset rather than duplicating the whole tree. Every CSS declaration in style.cljc references a token var, never a literal, and the design doc calls out two real bugs (an orphaned shadow rule, unreferenced rim tokens) that motivated moving to EDN-data rules specifically so token usage could be asserted in tests — this is unusually rigorous, intentional token governance for a UI library."}
{:eval/lib :liquid-glass-ui, :eval/axis :axis/token-discipline-judged, :eval/layer :llm-judge, :eval/score 5.00, :eval/judge "llm-judge-2", :eval/run-id "design-quality-20260713-0544", :eval/at "2026-07-13T05:44:03Z", :eval/seq 85, :eval/note "Tokens are organized into clearly-scoped groups with documented rationale for nearly every value: the spring easing is generated from an explicit damped-spring formula (not a magic literal) and documented with its ζ/ω/samples choice, the :specular :rim asymmetry is explained as \"light from above,\" dark-tokens is deliberately a partial override map with a stated reason each entry is scheme-sensitive, and the design doc even cites two real bugs (panel--flat's missing shadow rule, unreferenced rim tokens) that motivated moving CSS to EDN declaration data specifically so token usage could be asserted against in tests rather than regex-scraped. This reads as a genuinely systematic, self-auditing token pipeline."}
